WEBVTT

00:00:00.605 --> 00:00:30.237
<v Riley>Morning, good to have you. Today we've got the Williams Lake ballot record, with 1,466 advance votes and a new high for a single advance day. There's Sḱulé7ten Park, opened downtown with a name meaning "where you find yourself." And the Kodiak bear trip, a Pratesi story about tracking brown bears with Alutiiq guides on Kodiak Island. Then the Deep Dive: when wildfire and extreme heat hit the same people on the same days, who gets out, and who waits. Stick around.

00:00:43.712 --> 00:01:04.488
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that affect our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Friday, October 16th, 2026. Today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life, and we're reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:05.488 --> 00:01:32.872
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're heading past the pavement today, into fire country, bear country, and the places where people have to stay put when the wind shifts. Quick weather check: out at Horsefly Lake it's overcast and around 6 this morning, with a high of 19, light drizzle, and a 6 percent chance of precipitation. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 8 and overcast, Williams Lake is around 6 and partly cloudy, and Hanceville is around 5 and mainly clear.

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:35.213
<v Riley>Bring a rain shell. We'll start with the news.

00:01:36.613 --> 00:02:11.203
<v Riley>Most of today's news runs close to home, mostly about the civic vote and who gets a seat at the table in our own towns. Williams Lake set a new advance voting record. The city counted 1,466 ballots across the two advance days, 189 more than the 2022 advance period. Wednesday, October 14th, brought in 925 ballots on its own, the busiest advance day the Lake City has ever had. General voting day is Saturday, October 17th, from 8 a.m. to 8 p.m., at the Marie Sharpe Elementary School gymnasium on Cameron Street.

00:02:12.782 --> 00:02:40.390
<v Casey>Williams Lake residents also turned out for a free movie night at Boitanio Park on Wednesday evening. Williams Lake First Nation presented the screening of Minions &amp; Monsters, working with the Canadian Mental Health Association and Treko Metals. Families showed up with blankets, lawn chairs, and mats, and the big screen was one the Nation bought over the summer. Robin Willems, who works with CMHA's family and youth department, said free events help people mingle and cut down on isolation.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.368
<v Riley>A screen the Nation owns is a community asset that'll get used again.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:03:26.250
<v Riley>Williams Lake First Nation opened its memorial park downtown on Tuesday, October 13th, and unveiled the name Sḱulé7ten Park. According to WLFN's own post, the name is the traditional Secwepemcstin word for the area that makes up downtown Williams Lake, and it translates to "where you find yourself." The park honours the Nation's landmark Supreme Court Village claim victory and the lasting impact of the residential school system. William Lulua explained that the area was where his people went vision questing before the city grew around it. Elder Virginia Gilbert, who attended both day school and residential school, gave the opening prayer.

00:03:27.661 --> 00:04:02.387
<v Casey>From a park downtown to a hospital hallway. An open letter from South Okanagan physicians says Interior Health is driving away the only neurologists stationed at Penticton Regional Hospital. Three neurologists have filed resignations, finishing in November and December. They objected to a requirement for expanded after-hours on-call coverage beyond the South Okanagan, with no increase in pay. The letter also points to more neurologists leaving in Kamloops and Vernon, and to 2025, when four psychiatrists left Vernon Hospital and seven obstetrician-gynecologists left the Royal Inland Hospital in Kamloops.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:33.786
<v Riley>Back to the Williams Lake ballot. The Tyee reports that First Nations governments have made a rare endorsement in the Williams Lake mayoral race, backing Surinderpal Rathor. According to WLFN's coverage of the same decision, the Nation says Rathor has rebuilt a relationship that was damaged during Walt Cobb's time as mayor. The Nation points to statements and positions from Cobb's tenure that damaged trust with First Nation leadership and drew public criticism across the Cariboo.

00:04:35.238 --> 00:05:08.092
<v Casey>B.C.'s three main party leaders met in the only scheduled televised debate of the campaign on Wednesday evening. Interim BC Conservative Leader Lorne Doerkson, NDP Leader David Eby, and Green Leader Emily Lowan clashed over affordability, health care, and Indigenous rights. Eby defended calling an election two years into his government's mandate, arguing that the NDP's one-seat majority means almost any vote could topple it. CentreBC and OneBC did not meet the debate criteria, and their leaders delivered brief prerecorded statements near the end. The provincial vote is October 24th.

00:05:08.542 --> 00:05:12.510
<v Riley>A one-seat majority explains the early call, and it explains the tone.

00:05:14.166 --> 00:05:49.660
<v Riley>Quesnel council has pushed its centennial celebration and the gold pan repair or replacement to the next council. At the October 6th meeting, Mayor Ron Paull presented a report summarizing the governance and strategy committee's September 15th session. The centennial, slated for 2028, and the gold pan pricing will carry over into the 2027 operating plan. The report also flagged unsightly property complaints tied to bylaw staffing capacity, and traffic congestion came up at that same September meeting. Paull said most of the plan is on track.

00:05:51.251 --> 00:06:15.435
<v Casey>The Quesnel Cariboo Observer is running candidate questions for the District of Wells. Elyssia Sasaki, one of the councillor candidates, answered four questions, with up to 500 words each. She describes herself as an arts administrator, event producer, grantwriter, and non-profit worker. She frames the next council's job as balancing social, environmental, and economic sustainability. Her record in public office is not in today's sources.

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:41.348
<v Riley>Quesnel Live Arts opens its season today with the Cheng Squared Duo, a brother and sister team returning to Quesnel after a performance in January 2019. The organization is presenting six shows this year. Usually there's an extra one-off, but chair Colleen Brines says time and money meant six. Buzz Brass Plays Gershwin is on the list, and for the first time that quintet is adding a pianist.

00:06:42.863 --> 00:07:08.599
<v Casey>Staying with people who want to be out there, Outside Online ran a parent's account of hiking with a son who uses a wheelchair. More than two dozen National Parks sites now offer adaptive trail equipment, including all five of Utah's national parks. The family used a Cascade Tandem Hiking Cart on a Rocky Mountain trail. When the path turned to steps, a hiker helped lift the cart up, and the family kept going, instead of turning back the way they usually have to.

00:07:09.049 --> 00:07:11.393
<v Riley>Two dozen sites is a program, not a pilot.

00:07:13.202 --> 00:07:39.674
<v Riley>Outside's associate editor Madison Dapcevich looked into a ghost report on Idaho's Main Salmon River, the River of No Return. Several rafting groups reported seeing a woman in white along the banks this summer. Nate Lewand and his fleet spotted one at Black Canyon in July, a figure on a beach too small for a tent, dry in the rain, carrying no gear. Outside also spoke with a local historian about the river's pioneer women.

00:07:40.124 --> 00:07:42.396
<v Casey>Rafters seeing things in July rain. Okay.

00:07:44.181 --> 00:07:53.989
<v Casey>Outside also ran the story of a 72-year-old hiker who survived six nights in the backcountry after falling 100 feet. The piece is titled "She Had a Will to Live."

00:07:55.496 --> 00:08:23.360
<v Riley>Outside contributor Gwen Pratesi tracked Kodiak bears in Alaska with Alutiiq guides. The trip was on Kodiak Island, inside the Kodiak National Wildlife Refuge, and the lodge is Indigenous-owned. The article puts the population at around 3,500 bears, and says these bears have been isolated from other brown bears for about 12,000 years. The group went to the Thumb River in mid-August, one of the better bear-viewing spots on the island.

00:08:24.797 --> 00:08:44.925
<v Casey>Small Farm Canada reports that deer breeding season is pushing up collision risk on rural roads right now. In Ontario, about 12,000 wildlife collisions happen each year, with roughly 400 human injuries. Deer account for most of the reported incidents. October and November are the peak months, and dawn and dusk are the riskiest hours.

00:08:45.374 --> 00:08:47.134
<v Riley>Dawn and dusk is the commute out here.

00:08:48.961 --> 00:09:08.825
<v Riley>New York City's mayor, Zohran Mamdani, and Council Speaker Julie Menin reached a deal on Tuesday, October 13th, to outlaw horse-drawn carriage rides for hire. If the bill is enacted, the rides would end June 1st, 2028. Fast Company's timeline notes that earlier mayors pushed the issue, but none secured a full ban.

00:09:10.259 --> 00:09:24.963
<v Casey>Tom's Guide tested Nvidia's RTX Spark inside a Surface Laptop Ultra. The graphics hit 115 frames per second in DLSS gaming, and the lab run included battery tests. Their verdict is that Apple takes the CPU comparison, while Nvidia's GPU broke their charts.

00:09:25.413 --> 00:09:29.109
<v Riley>Battery life is the number that matters on a ridge with no outlet.

00:09:30.667 --> 00:09:57.779
<v Casey>An Adafruit Blog post from October 7th describes OFFgrid-SDR, a complete software-defined radio receiver in a single HTML file. You copy the file to a USB pen drive, add an RTL-SDR stick and an antenna, and you get shortwave, FM, airband, and VHF and UHF listening on most computers. It needs no installation, no administrator rights, and no internet connection. The builder's point is that the cheap RTL-SDR stick has always been an add-on, and this turns it into the main tool.

00:09:58.229 --> 00:10:02.301
<v Riley>A radio that works when the network's down is a backcountry tool first.

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:07.014
<v Riley>That's the news. Next, a local organization doing steady work for families.

00:10:08.414 --> 00:10:30.366
<v Riley>The Cariboo Family Enrichment Centre has been offering free family services in 100 Mile House since 1988. That includes counselling, prenatal support, and childcare resource and referral. Their Raven Youth Activity Centre gives teens a place to go. If your family could use a hand, or you know someone who could, you'll find them at cariboofamily.org.

00:10:30.992 --> 00:10:35.848
<v Casey>Free, local, and open for nearly four decades. That beats most of the press releases we get.

00:10:35.948 --> 00:10:40.156
<v Casey>Fire, bears, and a canyon wind give us plenty to pull on. Over to the deep end.

00:10:41.556 --> 00:11:12.420
<v Riley>When the smoke comes down the canyon, who gets to drive out, and who has to wait it out? Start with the number. Between 2000 and 2019, the count of people exposed to wildfire and extreme heat on the same days rose by 287 million. Inside Climate News calls it the largest global assessment of that overlap to date, published in Science Advances. The annual average went from about 761 million people in the first half of the period to over a billion in the second.

00:11:13.093 --> 00:11:28.117
<v Casey>That's modeled exposure. Same-day overlap isn't a health outcome. What share of the 287 million comes from hazard change, and what share from more people living in hot, fire-prone places? That split decides whether this is a climate story or a settlement story.

00:11:28.641 --> 00:11:45.985
<v Riley>Both are in it. A related Science Advances paper covered the Western US from 2006 to 2020 and mapped co-exposure to extreme heat, burn zones, and smoke at census-tract level. That's the resolution an evacuation planner needs. You can't warn a tract you haven't mapped.

00:11:46.585 --> 00:11:55.425
<v Casey>Mapping tells you a tract is exposed. It doesn't tell you whether anyone in it has a running vehicle, a bed to go to, or a neighbour who'll pick up the phone.

00:11:55.996 --> 00:12:09.148
<v Riley>Then the map is step one of three. Alerts are step two. Transport and shelter are step three, and that's where the planning usually runs out. Nobody argues against step three. The argument is about who pays for it.

00:12:09.758 --> 00:12:19.862
<v Casey>Take 100 Mile House, around 1,900 people. The highway closes and the power goes out on the same afternoon. Who runs the shelter, and who drives the people with no car?

00:12:20.127 --> 00:12:30.031
<v Riley>Planning can answer that. Name the shelter before the fire, pre-register the people who need rides, and rehearse it. Most small communities do this rarely, if at all.

00:12:30.357 --> 00:12:33.821
<v Casey>Rehearsal costs money every year. A binder doesn't get a driver.

00:12:34.142 --> 00:12:37.750
<v Riley>Then budget for the driver. That's a line item, not a mystery.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:54.250
<v Casey>Southern California is the live test of that line item. Red flag warnings went up for the first big Santa Ana event of the season, with the strongest gusts forecast through Friday. The National Weather Service called it a widespread event of elevated fire weather across the whole region.

00:12:54.877 --> 00:13:07.277
<v Riley>Mayor Karen Bass directed the fire department to stage personnel and equipment, and asked the state for emergency assistance. Electricity cutoffs were forecast too. That's a city moving before the wind arrives.

00:13:07.828 --> 00:13:22.476
<v Casey>Staging is also what you do after you've been blamed. The LA Times framed this as one of the first real tests of lessons from the Palisades and Eaton fires. The test isn't the trucks. It's whether people on the edge of the canyon heard the warning before the wind did.

00:13:23.056 --> 00:13:29.656
<v Riley>Trucks staged in advance are a learned response. Nobody pre-positions equipment for a wind event they've never had to fight.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:42.494
<v Casey>Or the mayor got a phone call from the state. Both can be true. Power cutoffs stop the lines from sparking, but they don't tell the person at the end of a gravel road that the wind is coming, or that they have no car to leave in.

00:13:43.054 --> 00:13:51.022
<v Riley>Alert design is solvable. Route the warning to the tracts the model flags, and use the channels people in those tracts actually use.

00:13:51.703 --> 00:14:00.359
<v Casey>Which channels? Phone platforms set their own rules for emergency messages. A warning that depends on a company's terms is only as reliable as those terms.

00:14:00.964 --> 00:14:06.996
<v Riley>Then the public agency should own the channel and publish the rules. That's public infrastructure, which beats none.

00:14:07.554 --> 00:14:12.202
<v Casey>Fine. Who holds the data, and who gets to read it? The Kodiak guides raise the same question.

00:14:12.746 --> 00:14:48.168
<v Riley>Prop 45 is the other half of this. It's on the November ballot, and it would amend CEQA, the state's landmark environmental review law. It sets deadlines for environmental review and for resolving lawsuits against approvals. It covers most housing, transportation, water, and health projects. The California Chamber of Commerce backs it. Opponents include environmental groups, some labor unions, and the California Democratic Party. They reported raising about $15.6 million, with major money from philanthropist Wendy Schmidt and several labor and environmental groups.

00:14:48.713 --> 00:15:01.753
<v Casey>The New York Times piece quotes Christopher Elmendorf, a land-use law professor at UC Davis, who says the Chamber is effectively asking for the growth California had in the 1960s. That's edge-city growth. And the edge is where the fire is.

00:15:02.264 --> 00:15:19.936
<v Riley>The strongest case for it is simple. CEQA lawsuits have made close-in infill slow and expensive. When building gets stuck inside the city, it moves to cheaper land, and the cheap land is the wildland-urban interface. Faster approvals reduce the pressure to push homes into the places that burn.

00:15:20.611 --> 00:15:31.827
<v Casey>Faster approvals don't stop builders from choosing fire-prone land. They make it quicker to get permits there. The clock speeds up everywhere, including the places where the risk is already on the record.

00:15:32.407 --> 00:15:46.039
<v Riley>The answer is in the structure, not the clock. A review that ends with a required fire-hardening plan differs from one that ends in a lawsuit. The question is whether review in fire-prone ground does real work or just adds years.

00:15:46.594 --> 00:15:51.898
<v Casey>Who writes that plan? And who checks it in year five, after the permit is issued and the lawyers have moved on?

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:56.584
<v Riley>The local government. The same people who'd check it under the old system.

00:15:57.101 --> 00:16:06.117
<v Casey>Same people, with a shorter clock and fewer lawsuits tying them up. The summary I have describes deadlines. It doesn't describe anything that waives conditions.

00:16:06.459 --> 00:16:10.131
<v Riley>Right. Deadlines, not waivers, and I wouldn't claim otherwise.

00:16:10.705 --> 00:16:21.641
<v Casey>Then we agree on the summary. Pro-housing groups, California YIMBY and SPUR, withheld support over sprawl and environmental risk. They're the people who want homes built, and they're still not sure where.

00:16:22.166 --> 00:16:30.742
<v Riley>Groups that want housing still disagree about where it goes, and a deadline doesn't answer that. It only sets how fast the answer arrives.

00:16:31.431 --> 00:16:41.143
<v Casey>Speed doesn't sort by fire risk. A town like Quesnel can defer a project by a year and call it prudence. A developer who wants to build on a ridge doesn't get that choice.

00:16:41.671 --> 00:16:47.687
<v Riley>A small town can defer. A state with a housing deficit can't wait the same way. That tension doesn't resolve neatly.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:52.256
<v Casey>Agreed. But I'd rather the review be slow and specific than fast and generic.

00:16:52.816 --> 00:17:19.264
<v Riley>The Kodiak piece puts a different kind of knowledge on the table. Outside contributor Gwen Pratesi went out with Alutiiq guides on Kodiak Island, inside the Kodiak National Wildlife Refuge. The lodge is Indigenous-owned. The bears there have been isolated from other brown bears for about 12,000 years, and the population is around 3,500. The guides know where the bears move, and they've known for generations.

00:17:19.798 --> 00:17:26.574
<v Casey>The people who can afford the trip see the bears. The people who live on the island year-round deal with a bear that comes up the road.

00:17:27.256 --> 00:17:38.832
<v Riley>That's a real access question. But the knowledge is the point. Guides keep a record of where animals move and when. Log those sightings with dates and feed them into the same map as the sensors.

00:17:39.398 --> 00:17:48.158
<v Casey>Who holds that log? A shared map on someone else's platform is a rented ledger. The knowledge stays with the people who made it, and the data leaves the land.

00:17:48.838 --> 00:17:54.998
<v Riley>Then own it locally. A regional district or a First Nation holds the data, and the platform only hosts it.

00:17:55.551 --> 00:18:03.591
<v Casey>Better. Still, a regional district carrying the liability for a bear sighting is a new job, and I don't know whether they've got the staff for it.

00:18:04.190 --> 00:18:25.142
<v Riley>Home shows the cost. The 100 Mile House black bear was euthanized after community sightings existed for weeks, and there was no formal channel to flag it early. We covered that on September 25th. The people who saw the bear were the ones who stayed in town, and the people with authority had no channel to receive what they saw.

00:18:25.706 --> 00:18:31.642
<v Casey>And the regional coordinator seats were empty. People saw the animal and nobody was paid to act on the sighting.

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:40.628
<v Riley>So the mandate has to be built into the record. A sighting log with a named owner, a response window, and a rule that says who has to answer.

00:18:41.186 --> 00:18:45.906
<v Casey>A response window with nobody funded to meet it is a press release with timestamps.

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:52.214
<v Riley>Then name the owner first. Regional district, Nation, or province. Name one, and the window can be enforced.

00:18:52.902 --> 00:18:59.038
<v Casey>I'd want that in writing before I'd believe it. And I'd want to see who answers the first time a sighting comes in after dark.

00:18:59.685 --> 00:19:10.797
<v Riley>Try a test that needs no grant. Pull the sighting reports from the last two years and count how many had a named person assigned to respond. If the answer is close to zero, you've found the gap.

00:19:11.338 --> 00:19:16.274
<v Casey>Useful, and cheap. It'll also tell us whether the people who stayed were ever counted at all.

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:23.706
<v Riley>I'd still argue the sensors belong in it. The bear was a documented problem for weeks. A shared log makes the weeks visible.

00:19:24.221 --> 00:19:30.773
<v Casey>I'll give you the log. I won't give you that a log makes anyone act. The Chamber of Commerce can publish a timeline too.

00:19:31.353 --> 00:19:41.129
<v Riley>We still disagree on Prop 45. I think faster review is the right tool for housing, and fire risk is a reason to review well, not a reason to never build.

00:19:41.695 --> 00:19:48.807
<v Casey>I think it's a developer's tool with a housing label, and it's going to get tested first where the fire already knows where it's going.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:56.912
<v Riley>So the question for any Cariboo council is one we can act on. Who owns the warning, and does it reach the person who can't drive out?

00:19:57.233 --> 00:20:06.769
<v Casey>And after the wind drops, who checks whether that person ever heard it? Tell us what your town's plan looks like at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Enjoy your weekend.

00:20:06.949 --> 00:20:08.149
<v Riley>Bring the rain shell.